From: Senemos Maintainers <75160848+MCC45TR@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: qcom-hw: start LMH feedback from policy ready

CPUFreq calls ready after initial policy setup and inactive-policy
reactivation. Patch 0055 queues polling from online but retains ready's
direct enable_irq. A real release-116 CPU7 cycle lets the worker and ready
enable the same IRQ, producing an unbalanced-enable warning. Its original
four-callback fixture omitted ready; the claimed general reactivation gap
in 0055's description is superseded by this full caller analysis.

Record the IRQF_NO_AUTOEN reference after a successful IRQ request. Online
only restores affinity and clears cancellation. Ready starts feedback with
the single reference held; the existing poller releases it after recovery.
An IRQ request failure leaves an inactive sentinel, so later callbacks and
exit cannot operate on an unowned IRQ.

Compared Linux v7.3.1 79a907609d53d5cd645b42c11550d295d43abf99,
current upstream cbbddb6b08cc9900a87cccaa9b22bbe06aa4f50a,
MiCode Uke 5712080a4b0c0b1a879e6d145c601c649ddaddfd and OnePlus
SM7675 fc892483f1d61f245924681eb5abd563564d9cba. Their ready callbacks
retain direct IRQ enabling. Linux GPL attribution is unchanged. No board
values, frequencies, voltages, protection thresholds or donor code change.

Validation: the expanded actual-function ASan/UBSan fixture reproduces the
release-116 duplicate enable and passes after this correction. It covers
initial activation, ready/poll ordering, IRQ-request failure, teardown and
128 clipping/offline/online/recovery cycles. Kernel and physical acceptance
remain separate.

--- a/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
@@ -477,8 +477,11 @@
 				   IRQF_ONESHOT | IRQF_NO_AUTOEN, data->irq_name, data);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(&pdev->dev, "Error registering %s: %d\n", data->irq_name, ret);
+		data->throttle_irq = 0;
 		return 0;
 	}
+	/* IRQF_NO_AUTOEN holds the initial disable until policy ready. */
+	data->throttle_irq_disabled = true;
 
 	ret = irq_set_affinity_and_hint(data->throttle_irq, policy->cpus);
 	if (ret)
@@ -506,9 +509,6 @@
 
 	mutex_lock(&data->throttle_lock);
 	data->cancel_throttle = false;
-	/* Refresh feedback before releasing the one retained IRQ disable. */
-	if (data->throttle_irq_disabled)
-		mod_delayed_work(system_highpri_wq, &data->throttle_work, 0);
 	mutex_unlock(&data->throttle_lock);
 
 	return ret;
@@ -619,8 +619,14 @@
 {
 	struct qcom_cpufreq_data *data = policy->driver_data;
 
-	if (data->throttle_irq >= 0)
-		enable_irq(data->throttle_irq);
+	if (data->throttle_irq <= 0)
+		return;
+
+	mutex_lock(&data->throttle_lock);
+	/* The poller owns initial activation and policy reactivation. */
+	if (!data->cancel_throttle && data->throttle_irq_disabled)
+		mod_delayed_work(system_highpri_wq, &data->throttle_work, 0);
+	mutex_unlock(&data->throttle_lock);
 }
 
 static struct cpufreq_driver cpufreq_qcom_hw_driver = {
